    {
      "filename": "Dendera Temple complex (2007-05-311) (1241076587).jpg",
      "title": "File:Dendera Temple complex (2007-05-311) (1241076587).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/Dendera_Temple_complex_%282007-05-311%29_%281241076587%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dendera_Temple_complex_(2007-05-311)_(1241076587).jpg",
      "width": 2048,
      "height": 1365,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Vyacheslav Argenberg from Seattle, WA, USA",
      "description": "The whole complex covers some 40,000 square meters and is surrounded by a hefty mud brick enclosed wall. Dendera was a site for chapels or shrines from the beginning of history of ancient Egypt. It seems that pharaoh Pepi I (ca. 2250 BC) built on this site and evidence exists of a temple in the eighteenth dynasty (ca 1500 BC). But the earliest extant building in the compound today is the Mammisi raised by Nectanebo II – last of the native pharaohs (360-343 BC). The features in the complex include: Hathor temple (the main temple), Temple of the birth of Isis, Sacred Lake, Sanatorium, Mammisi of Nectanebo II, Christian Basilica, Roman Mammisi, a Bark shine, Gateways of Domitian &amp; Trajan and the Roman Kiosk.",
